\date{2025-11-29T19:09:08Z}
\title{Example: factorial}

\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{As another example, let's implement factorial (although it is already
built in).  Recall that #{n! = n \cdot (n-1) \cdot (n-2) \cdot \dots
\cdot 1}.  Another way to write this is ##{n! = n \cdot (n-1)!} If
we add a "base case" #{0! = 1}, this becomes a perfectly viable way
to \em{define} factorial.  We could transcribe it into [[0027]] as follows:
\pre\verb<<<|
fac : N -> N
fac(0) = 1
fac(n) = n * fac (n-1)
<<<
Unfortunately, this is a type error. The reason is that since the
input to \tt{fac} is supposed to be a natural number, that means
#{n-1} must be a natural number, but it cannot be since it [uses
subtraction](002I). We can use the \tt{.-} operator instead, which works
on [natural numbers](002D) (you will explore what this operator does on a
homework assignment).
\pre\verb<<<|
fac : N -> N
fac(0) = 1
fac(n) = n * fac (n .- 1)
<<<
}